# Claims manually read and formulated. A record can contain several formally
# equivalent clauses of one theorem, but distinct side claims get separate rows.
def c(key,item,scans,start,end,statement,status,cls,fate,refs,comp='No separate numerical computation; the cited geometric/logical argument was read.',file=P,qline=None,confidence='high',date=None,searches=None):
    return Cc(key,[item] if isinstance(item,str) else item,53 if file in (J1,J2,J3,J4,J5) else 46,scans,[loc(file,start,end)],quote(file,qline or start),statement,status,comp,cls,fate,date or {'first_circulation':'Not established from this source unless specified in fate.','publication':'See reference metadata; undated manuscript is not assigned a priority date.'},refs,confidence,searches)
c('rigidified_torsors','rigidified',[5,6,7],69,138,'For a group sheaf G on a site and endomorphism f, a right G-torsor P with φ(pg)=φ(p)f(g) is locally described by a∈G; changing trivialization by u gives b=uaf(u)^{-1}. If the associated Lang map is locally surjective, these objects form a neutral gerbe equivalent to torsors under Eq(f,id).','proof sketched; the intervening displayed noncommutative simplification is false, treated separately.','A','The torsor/descent construction belongs to the published SGA torsor formalism; the corrected coordinate calculation proves this precise local classification.','SGA1Torsors SGA3I'.split(),comp='checks.py: rigidified_S3 and free_word_twisting; proofs.md §1.',qline=87)
c('bad_rigidified_equation','rigidified_algebra',[6],110,133,'The literal implication ua=b f(u) ⇒ b^{-1}a=f(u)u^{-1} is false for noncommutative G. Take f=id and noncommuting u,a∈S3, and set b=uau^{-1}; the hypothesis holds and the alleged conclusion would force b=a.','false as stated; no local correction of this displayed equation on the supplied page.','C','Refuted as written; the corrected conjugacy formula is in CL key rigidified_torsors and is standard torsor descent.',['SGA3I'],comp='checks.py: rigidified_S3 supplies actual permutations and products.',qline=122)
c('lang_etale','lang',[7],140,171,'Let G/S be smooth and f:G→G an S-group endomorphism with df=0. The map x↦f(x)x^{-1} is étale. If every geometric fibre is connected, it is surjective.','proof sketched; explicitly asks to extract the lemma.','E','Lang proved the finite-field form in 1956; the zero-differential extension follows from the same open-orbit proof. The arbitrary-base conclusion is fibrewise with the smooth/l.f.p. hypothesis.',['Lang1956','SGA3VI_A'],comp='proofs.md §2 rechecks the tangent map and disjoint open twisted-conjugacy orbits.',qline=153,date={'first_circulation':'received 9 March 1956 (Lang article); no earlier circulation established','publication':'1956'})
c('fixed_points_finiteness','fixed_subgroup',[7],153,170,'Fixed points of f form an étale subgroup when df=0; for Frobenius on a finite-type F_q-group they form a finite constant group after the usual base extension. Without finite type or quasi-compactness, étale need not mean finite.','proof sketched; the unqualified finite wording needs its ambient finite-type convention.','C','The finite-type theorem is established. An infinite constant group over F_q has Frobenius=id and infinitely many fixed points, refuting unrestricted finiteness.',['Lang1956','Stacks047J'],comp='proofs.md §2 gives the infinite constant-group counterexample and checks all hypotheses.',qline=155)
c('quotient_open','fixed_subgroup',[7],155,164,'For the étale Lang map with kernel N=Eq(f,id), the quotient sheaf G/N maps monomorphically and étale to G and is therefore represented by an open subscheme; it is all of G in the connected-fibre case.','proof sketched.','A','The conclusion is a standard étale monomorphism/open immersion consequence within SGA descent; it does not require N to be finite.',['SGA3I','Stacks025F'],comp='proofs.md §2 checks equality of Lang values modulo the appropriate left/right N-action.',qline=157)
c('unit_frobenius_equivalence','unit_frob',[8,9],175,217,'On an F_p-scheme S, rank-n locally free O_S-modules with an isomorphism F_S^*E≅E are equivalent to locally constant étale sheaves of n-dimensional F_p-vector spaces.','proof sketched via GL_n and Lang.','A','This is the finite-rank unit-Frobenius/local-system construction; the rank and invertibility hypotheses match the manuscript and the SGA Frobenius formalism.',['Lang1956','SGA5XV'],comp='checks.py: frobenius_fixed_basis illustrates the scalar equation; proofs.md §3 gives the torsor argument.',qline=184)
c('frobenius_fixed_module_question','module_finiteness',[9],214,217,'For a finitely presented module M over an arbitrary characteristic-p ring R, an isomorphism F_R^*M≅M forces M locally free; without finite presentation the conclusion can fail even for cyclic modules.','open on the pages; the finite-presentation refinement and counterexample are editorial, not the author\'s stated theorem.','B','Finite-presentation refinement follows from base change of Fitting ideals and Nakayama. The unrestricted finite-generation version is false over a perfect non-noetherian ring; this is independently checked here.',['Stacks07Z6','Stacks0EBU'],comp='proofs.md §3: Fitting proof and R=∪F_p[t^(1/p^n)] localized at the positive-valuation ideal, M=R/m; checks.py: perfect_valuation_chain.',qline=214)
c('relative_frobenius_unramified','relative_frob',[11],234,242,'For X→S locally of finite presentation in characteristic p, X→S is unramified iff F_(X/S) is a monomorphism (equivalently unramified).','proved there, with a diagonal ideal calculation.','A','Published in the EGA/SGA Frobenius criteria; the local finite-presentation qualification is essential to the unramified terminology used.',['Stacks025F','SGA5XV'],comp='checks.py: frobenius_ideal powers; proofs.md §4 explains the diagonal criterion.',qline=234)
c('relative_frobenius_etale','relative_frob',[11],244,276,'For X→S locally of finite presentation in characteristic p, X→S is étale iff its relative Frobenius is an isomorphism.','proved there.','A','The exact criterion is established in EGA/SGA and the Stacks Frobenius section.',['Stacks025F','SGA5XV'],comp='No numerical computation; diagonal/flatness argument checked against the published criterion.',qline=244)
c('frobenius_ideal','relative_frob',[11,19],270,276,'Under Frobenius an ideal J maps to J^[p]=(a^p:a∈J). If J^p denotes the ordinary ideal power, the asserted equality is false, e.g. xy∈(x,y)^2 but xy∉(x²,y²) in F_2[x,y].','false as an ordinary-ideal-power reading; the intended notation is unresolved without scans.','C','Frobenius powers give the valid statement. The discrepancy is elementary and does not refute the relative-Frobenius theorem.',['SGA5XV','Stacks0CC6'],comp='checks.py: frobenius_ideal; complete monomial membership certificate.',qline=274)
c('frobenius_basic','projection_ver',[12,13],279,337,'Absolute Frobenius is natural; the relative Frobenius X→X^(p/S) is integral, radicial and surjective, hence a universal homeomorphism, and is compatible with base change.','proved there or checked in the formularium.','A','The same formulas appear in SGA5 XV §1; the projection named Ver is not the commutative-group Verschiebung.',['SGA5XV','Stacks0CC6'],comp='checks.py: frobenius_ring_formulas; proofs.md §4 distinguishes absolute, relative and projection maps.',qline=317)
c('frobenius_module_functor','frob_functors',[13,14],347,423,'Base change by Frobenius preserves quasi-coherence, finite generation/presentation, local freeness, flat modules, symmetric algebras and tensor products; it is right exact but not generally left exact or compatible with arbitrary Hom.','stated with elementary justifications; limitations noted.','A','These are general base-change properties. In the noetherian case, Frobenius itself is flat exactly for regular rings (Kunz).',['SGA5XV','Stacks0EBU'],comp='checks.py: nonflat_frobenius on F_p[ε]/ε²; symmetric-power identity by universal property in proofs.md §4.',qline=365)
c('frobenius_projective','linebundle_frob',[14,15],375,441,'Frobenius base change commutes with Spec and Proj of the relevant algebra, and the relative p-power map on projective space pulls O(1) back to O(p). For every invertible sheaf L, F^*L≅L^⊗p.','proved there by the homogeneous and transition-function construction.','A','The exact formulas are in SGA5 XV §1; no characteristic-zero analogue is asserted.',['SGA5XV'],comp='checks.py: transition_cocycle_p and homogeneous_degrees; proofs.md §4.',qline=437)
c('frobenius_coefficients','linebundle_frob',[15],442,470,'The p-power map on coordinates is additive in characteristic p, but is linear only after the appropriate Frobenius twist; the formulas are natural for base change and composition.','proved there by formulas.','A','This is the standard Frobenius-semilinear formularium, published in SGA5 XV §1.',['SGA5XV'],comp='checks.py: freshman_dream and semilinear_scalar; includes a scalar in F_4 witnessing failure of ordinary F_4-linearity.',qline=442)
c('iterated_twists','frob_iterates',[16,17],473,512,'Over a perfect field, a Frobenius twist has an isomorphic underlying scheme via the automorphism of the base; an F_(p^h)-structure returns after an h-fold Frobenius twist. This is not in general an isomorphism over the original base field before iteration.','proof sketched; final corollary unfinished and indices rewritten.','A','The valid iteration identities are in the SGA Frobenius formalism. The literal inconsistent h/ν indices and unfinished corollary cannot identify a further theorem.',['SGA5XV'],comp='checks.py: finite_field_F4_iterate; proofs.md §4 clarifies the category of each map.',qline=487)
c('frobenius_kernel_cancelled','cancelled_kernel',[17],524,530,'The draft proposition asserting finiteness/radiciality/surjectivity of a Frobenius kernel or map is cancelled; no live theorem with all of its wording survives.','corrected by himself later: cancelled at scan 17; different live height statement at scan 19.','F','The cancelled formulation is not identifiable as a retained assertion. The related correct height-neighbourhood claim is separately indexed.',['SGA5XV','SGA3VIIA'],qline=528,confidence='medium')
c('homeomorphism_affine','affine_homeomorphism',[18],533,565,'A morphism of schemes which is a homeomorphism onto a closed subset is affine; in particular a morphism which is a homeomorphism onto the target is affine. No finite-presentation assumption is needed.','proved there by a replacement proof; the first proof is cancelled.','A','The precise lemma remains established (Stacks Lemma 29.45.4, tag 04DE). The manuscript replacement is a valid local-affine argument.',['Stacks04DE'],comp='proofs.md §5 rechecks the replacement argument; no numerical computation.',qline=534)
c('height_frobenius','height_neighbourhood',[19],567,581,'For an affine group G=Spec A over a characteristic-p base with augmentation ideal I, the ν-th relative Frobenius kernel is Spec A/I^[p^ν]; Frobenius-height≤ν means each element of I has p^ν-th power zero. The construction only needs the marked section.','proved there with the preceding affineness lemma.','A','This is the established infinitesimal group/Frobenius-neighbourhood construction. Ordinary powers give a different neighbourhood.',['SGA3VIIA','SGA5XV'],comp='checks.py: frobenius_neighbourhood_lengths compares lengths p^(2ν) and q(q+1)/2 for G_a².',qline=570)
c('global_units_p','picp_units',[20],584,602,'For proper reduced X over a perfect field k of characteristic p, Γ(X,O_X) is a finite reduced k-algebra and Frobenius is bijective on it; hence Γ(X,O_X^*) is p-divisible and Pic(X)[p]≅H^1(X,O_X^*/O_X^{*p}).','proved there via the long exact sequence.','A','The assertion follows from proper global-function finiteness and the unit-power exact sequence; it is weaker in hypotheses than the subsequent differential claim.',['StacksProperFunctions','Oda1969'],comp='proofs.md §6 writes the exact sequence and removes the global-unit cokernel.',qline=584)
c('dlog_injective','picp_dlog',[20,21],603,628,'For normal integral X in characteristic p, a rational p-th root of a regular function is regular, so dlog induces the indicated injection from O_X^*/O_X^{*p} into rational differentials. The induced Pic[p]→global differential map needs the exact sheaf sequence used on the page.','question at scan20, proof sketched at scan21.','B','Oda §5 explicitly verifies the kernel of dlog for normal schemes. The global smooth-proper Cartier-fixed identification is a separate precise theorem.',['Oda1969','Cartier1958'],comp='proofs.md §6 checks integrality of a p-th root and the connecting map.',qline=625)
c('cartier_fixed_picp','picp_dlog',[21],629,648,'For smooth proper X over a perfect field of characteristic p, Pic(X)[p] is identified by dlog with the global closed one-forms ω satisfying Cω=ω; local Cartier-fixed forms are logarithmic differentials.', 'proof sketched; a normal-to-regular reduction in the literal text is not justified.','E','Cartier\'s 1958 logarithmic criterion supplies the local statement; Oda1969 explains the global differential framework and its limits. This does not identify the whole p-torsion Picard group scheme from rational points.',['Cartier1958','Oda1969'],comp='proofs.md §6; checks.py: dlog_p_power verifies the denominator obstruction.',qline=629,date={'first_circulation':'Cartier thesis 1958; earlier circulation not established','publication':'1958 (Cartier); 1969 (Oda)'})
c('bad_dlog_quotient','dlog_quotient',[21],640,649,'The literal map K^*/O^*→Ω_K^1/Ω_O^1 is not injective: in O=k[t]_(t), f=t^p is nonunit but dlog(f)=0. Divide the source additionally by K^{*p} for a possible injective formulation.','false as stated; the missing quotient is not supplied in the transcription.','C','Refuted. The corrected Cartier/logarithmic statements are established; extending them from smooth to arbitrary normal differentials requires separate hypotheses.',['Cartier1958','Oda1969'],comp='checks.py: dlog_p_power; valuation p>0 certifies f∉O*.')
c('restricted_p_transpose','transpose_cartier',[22],654,676,'The restricted power D↦D^p on derivations is additive only with Jacobson correction and is not O-linear. Hence a naive O-linear transpose to Ω¹→Sym^p Ω¹ cannot give it; a Cartier-type operation on closed forms is the relevant refinement.','open on the pages.','B','Cartier1958 establishes the differential identity with its correction term; the plain linear-transpose formulation is impossible.',['Cartier1958','SGA3VIIA'],comp='checks.py: restricted_derivations; proofs.md §7 compares D^p=0 and (tD)^p=tD.',qline=654)
c('height_one_dictionary','height_one_duality',[22],679,688,'Finite locally free height-one group schemes in characteristic p correspond to finite locally free restricted Lie algebras (with the appropriate dual convention); the arbitrary quasi-coherent formulation written here needs more hypotheses.','stated without proof, as a proposed dictionary.','B','SGA3 VIIA Theorem7.4/Remark7.5 gives the flat finite-presentation equivalence and explicitly warns about nonflat variants.',['SGA3VIIA'],comp='No numerical computation; the finite-flat duality hypotheses are compared exactly.',qline=679)
c('height_one_commutativity','height_one_duality',[22],684,688,'Within the finite-flat height-one restricted-Lie equivalence, G is commutative iff the Lie bracket is zero, equivalently the dual map Ω→Λ²Ω vanishes.','stated without proof.','B','This is part of the restricted-Lie correspondence; it does not classify arbitrary nonflat group schemes using a dual module.',['SGA3VIIA'],qline=688)
c('verschiebung_symmetric','verschiebung',[24,25,26,27],2,77,'For a commutative affine group over a field of characteristic p, the p-fold invariant-tensor construction yields V:G^(p)→G. Mixed symmetric tensor orbits vanish under the diagonal p-power quotient, allowing the factorization.','proved there by a basis/orbit calculation.', 'B','SGA3 VIIA §4.3 develops the same construction, including finite-flat Cartier-dual compatibility; the field assumption in this letter is explicit.',['SGA3VIIA'],comp='checks.py: symmetric_orbits; the orbit-count divisibility argument is given in proofs.md §8.',file=C,qline=46)
c('verschiebung_FV','verschiebung',[26,27],43,77,'For the commutative group and its Frobenius twist, VF=[p] and FV=[p]; for a finite locally free commutative group, Cartier duality interchanges F and V.','proved there or stated at the end of the construction.','B','SGA3 VIIA4.3.1–4.3.3 gives the identities with the correct twists and finite-flat duality.',['SGA3VIIA'],comp='checks.py: FV_Ga_Gm_alpha; proofs.md §8 checks domains and the tensor pairing.',file=C,qline=74)
c('verschiebung_base_extension','verschiebung',[24,26],8,77,'The author leaves extension of the symmetric-tensor construction beyond a field to study of q∘i; no general base-ring theorem is proved in this typescript.','open on the pages.','B','Flat-group and finite-locally-free variants are established in SGA3 VIIA §4.3; the unqualified nonflat extension is not inferred.',['SGA3VIIA'],file=C,qline=46,confidence='medium')
c('uniform_axioms','uniform',[23],693,729,'The entourage-filter axioms for a uniformity are equivalent to the displayed symmetric/refinement basis axioms once W∘W⊂V is read as containment.','proof sketched; the relation-composition glyph is type-inconsistent literally.','E','This is standard uniform-space theory; the corrected axiom is independently rechecked in proofs.md. The reused sheet is not securely attributed.',['BourbakiUniform'],comp='proofs.md §9 shows the two refinement constructions; checks.py: finite_uniformity_axiom verifies the typing distinction.',qline=711)
c('frobenius_etale_comparison','etale_frob',[30,31,32],762,843,'The relative Frobenius of every étale U→X is an isomorphism; this defines a natural comparison between absolute-Frobenius pullback and the identity functor on X_et, compatible with base change.', 'proved there by a functorial construction.','A','Published in SGA5 XV §1 and restated in Stacks64.3 with the canonical coefficient comparison.',['SGA5XV','Stacks03SL'],comp='proofs.md §10 checks the site construction and naturality.',qline=795)
c('frobenius_nonconstant_sheaf','etale_frob',[32],833,843,'Even for X=Spec F_p where the scheme Frobenius is identity, the canonical comparison on an étale sheaf need not be identity: the finite étale object Spec F_(p²) carries nontrivial p-Frobenius.', 'stated with a qualification on constant sheaves.','A','The finite-field coefficient action is part of the standard arithmetic/geometric Frobenius distinction.',['Stacks03SL'],comp='checks.py: finite_field_F4_iterate; the nontrivial swap is explicit.',qline=842)
c('frobenius_cohomology','etale_frob',[33],845,878,'Using the canonical coefficient comparison, absolute Frobenius acts as identity on H^0, nonabelian H^1, abelian H^i and the stated bounded-above derived global sections.','proved there by H^0 plus derived functor/Yoneda reasoning.','A','The étale cohomological identity is published in SGA5 XV and Stacks64.3. It makes no triviality assertion about geometric Frobenius with its distinct finite-field comparison.',['SGA5XV','Stacks03SL'],comp='proofs.md §10 checks the H^0 natural transformation and its derived extension.',qline=866)
c('topos_b_implies_a','topos_axioms',[34],880,906,'In the displayed site setup, a natural base map whose pullback squares on designated étale objects are Cartesian induces the intended topos equivalence.','proof sketched.','A','The scheme case is exactly the published Frobenius argument. For the unspecified abstract category, the needed topology/subcanonical assumptions must be retained.',['SGA5XV','Stacks03SL'],qline=905,confidence='medium')
c('topos_converse','topos_axioms',[36],908,928,'The author asks whether topos equivalence (a) implies the relative Cartesian condition (b), and suggests requiring (a) after all base changes; an independence-of-topology clause is cancelled.','open on the pages; one stronger version asserted without a full proof.','F','No uniquely quantified abstract theorem can be identified from the incomplete site hypotheses. Searches located the scheme Frobenius theorem, not an unrestricted converse; fppf μ_p shows that changing topology is substantive.',['SGA5XV','SGA3VIIA'],comp='proofs.md §10 gives the μ_p topology boundary, not a proof of the unspecified converse.',qline=918,confidence='low')
c('gluing_H0','etale_frob',[35],78,99,'An unfinished gluing argument reduces a bijectivity assertion on H^0 from a sheaf F to an auxiliary G and an equalizer presentation; the surrounding category F and all maps are not supplied.','proof sketched; the local bijectivity step is completed on this page, but its full theorem is missing.','F','The standard sheaf equalizer principle is established; the absent global setup prevents identifying a stronger theorem from this fragment.',['SGA4Sheaves'],file=C,qline=88,confidence='low')
c('gluing_cohomology','etale_frob',[37],100,126,'A continuation asserts a cohomological gluing criterion for torsion coefficients under preceding hypotheses, using an embedding F→G and the pair G⇉G⊔_F G.','proof sketched; fragment ends at a page boundary.','F','Without the missing hypotheses the exact cohomological theorem is unidentifiable. The word conoyau for an embedding/equalizer step is a reading alarm, not a published identification.',['SGA4Sheaves'],comp='proofs.md §10 checks the elementary equalizer/cokernel direction.',file=C,qline=125,confidence='low')
c('bs_geometry','bs_split',[41],950,965,'An fppf form of P^n_S is smooth and proper over S; its anticanonical line bundle descends and is relatively ample, giving the usual projectivity assertion under the chosen convention.', 'proof sketched by descent.','A','Published in Grothendieck\'s Brauer I/II treatment of Brauer–Severi schemes.',['BrauerI1966','BrauerII1966'],qline=959)
c('bs_degree_one','bs_split',[41],961,980,'A Brauer–Severi scheme P/S is isomorphic to P(E) for a vector bundle E iff it has a line bundle restricting to O(1) on geometric fibres; a section of P/S implies this condition by the incidence construction.', 'proved there/sketched incidence argument.','A','Grothendieck Brauer II §0 records precisely the degree-one and section splitting criteria.',['BrauerII1966'],comp='proofs.md §11 checks descent of O(1), pushforward and the section/incidence argument.',qline=961)
c('coxeter_graph','coxeter',[44],1030,1057,'For a finite real reflection group, the Coxeter graph is independent of the chosen chamber up to isomorphism; irreducibility of the representation corresponds to connectedness of the graph, and the graph has no circuits.', 'proof sketched on the reused sheet.','E','These are classical finite Coxeter-system facts, recorded in the opened standard reflection-group source; attribution/date of the reused sheet is unknown.',['Humphreys1990'],comp='proofs.md §12 gives the positive-definite Gram-matrix obstruction to a cycle.',qline=1042)
c('forest_lemma','coxeter',[46],1085,1107,'A finite nonempty forest has a vertex of degree≤1; a finite forest is bipartite.','proved there, with an inserted correction sinon.','E','Elementary graph facts underlying the Coxeter argument; independently rechecked by induction and finite enumeration.',['Humphreys1990'],comp='checks.py: forests up to five vertices; proofs.md §12 supplies the all-size induction.',qline=1100)
c('coxeter_conjugacy','coxeter',[42,44],982,998,'All Coxeter elements of a finite Coxeter system are conjugate; cyclic rotation of a product conjugates it, and adjacent commuting generators can be interchanged.', 'proof sketched across scans46→44→42.','E','Classical Coxeter theory; the combinatorial proof needs the forest reduction, not just existence of a commuting pair.',['Humphreys1990'],comp='checks.py: coxeter_free_word_rotation and Sn_generators; proofs.md §12 explains the forest/source-to-sink move.',qline=990)
c('symmetric_generators','coxeter',[42],993,998,'An n-cycle and a transposition of two consecutive elements generate S_n; the order of a Coxeter element is the Coxeter number in the finite irreducible setting.', 'stated with an elementary argument/reference context.','E','The permutation assertion is elementary and classical; the Coxeter-number terminology is standard in finite reflection-group theory.',['Humphreys1990'],comp='checks.py: Sn_generators for n=2,…,7; proofs.md §12 gives the conjugate-adjacent-transposition proof.',qline=995)
c('bs_generic_injection','bs_generic',[43,57],1000,1015,'For an integral locally noetherian regular base, a generically split Brauer–Severi scheme is a projective bundle. A strictly locally factorial variant is obtained under the exact hypotheses of the Brauer cohomological injection.', 'stated with proof indicated by extending divisor classes.','A','Grothendieck Brauer II1.8 and Cor1.10 establish the relevant injection; the notes\' stronger factorial wording must be tied to those hypotheses.',['BrauerII1966'],qline=1006)
c('bs_obstruction_class','bs_obstruction',[43],1000,1015,'The connecting map H^1(PGL_n)→H^2(G_m) sends a Brauer–Severi scheme to the obstruction to lifting it to a vector bundle; vanishing is equivalent to P(E).', 'stated without full topology specification.','A','Published Brauer I/II gives the obstruction in the étale/fppf setting and distinguishes its image from the whole cohomology group.',['BrauerI1966','BrauerII1966'],comp='checks.py: central_cocycle_additivity; proofs.md §11 writes the Čech lift obstruction.',qline=1003)
c('linear_system_morphisms','rational_linear',[43,45],1019,1068,'A morphism X→P(E) is a generating invertible quotient of the pullback of E, equivalently a basepoint-free linear system with the chosen projective convention. Rational maps require a specified dense domain and base locus.', 'stated without full proof; rational variant lacks conditions.','A','The precise morphism statement is the universal property of projective bundles in EGA II/Stacks; the unrestricted rational-map phrase must be refined.',['Stacks01OA'],comp='proofs.md §11 checks the quotient convention and the rational base-locus qualification.',qline=1060)
c('systlin_picard','systlin',[45,47],1072,1132,'For an appropriate proper flat family with universal f_*O=O and cohomology/base-change conditions, the fibre of the linear-system functor over a Picard class is a (possibly twisted) projectivization of sections. A canonical section of λ is not specified in the literal text.', 'proof sketched for fibres; canonical-section assertion not well defined.','C','The relative Picard/divisor formalism is established in FGA; the unspecified empty-system convention and weakened hypotheses are not resolved by that identification.',['FGA236','BrauerII1966'],qline=1074,confidence='medium')
c('linear_mono_gap','linear_embedding',[47],1119,1126,'An arbitrary monomorphism P^r→P(Q) need not be a linear projective subspace. The Veronese map [s:t]↦[s²:st:t²] is a closed immersion but pulls O(1) back to O(2). A degree-one condition repairs the intended inference.', 'false as an inference from monomorphism alone; the full intended splitting proof may carry extra degree data.','C','The corrected degree-one criterion is standard projective geometry; the counterexample blocks only the truncated implication.',['Stacks01OA','BrauerII1966'],comp='checks.py: Veronese_relation; proofs.md §11.',qline=1122)
c('picard_section_splitting','bs_obstruction',[47],1128,1132,'When the relative Picard obstruction class becomes trivial after pullback to X and X→S has a section, that section pulls a trivialization back to S, so the associated Brauer–Severi obstruction vanishes.', 'proof sketched in a margin.','A','This is the standard functorial Brauer obstruction argument within the Picard framework, provided the same class is being compared.',['BrauerII1966','FGA236'],comp='proofs.md §11 writes s^*f^*=id and keeps the sign/dual convention separate.',qline=1128)
c('weakened_picard_hypotheses','weakened_flatness',[47,49],1124,1126,'The author proposes replacing flatness by universal O_S≅f_*O_X and finite presentation in part of the Picard/linear-system argument.', 'open on the pages; a line-bundle descent step continues at scan49.','C','Projection-formula descent steps can hold under quasi-compact/quasi-separated hypotheses, but they do not yield unrestricted relative Picard representability. The exact weaker theorem remains only partly formulated.',['FGA236','StacksProjection'],qline=1124,confidence='medium')
c('pid_divisible','pid_injective',[48],1134,1152,'For a module M over a PID R, multiplication by every nonzero a∈R is surjective iff Hom_R(-,M) is exact, equivalently M is injective.', 'stated without proof; refers to a prior problem.','E','The assertion is the PID case of Baer\'s criterion and was classical before these notes. The independent proof handles every ideal (a).',['StacksInjective'],comp='proofs.md §13 provides a complete Baer-criterion argument; no numerical computation.',qline=1138)
c('linebundle_descent_49','weakened_flatness',[49],2,13,'The continuation uses universal f_*O_X=O_S to descend a line-bundle comparison from X×_S P to P, through the projection formula and the unit/counit maps.', 'proof sketched; not a standalone general Picard representability theorem.','A','The projection-formula step is standard under the quasi-compact/quasi-separated and line-bundle hypotheses. Its scope is narrower than the earlier proposed removal of flatness.',['StacksProjection','FGA236'],comp='proofs.md §11 checks the unit map after pushforward.',file=K1,qline=8,confidence='medium')
c('padic_haar','haar',[50],14,19,'Z_p is the compact inverse limit of Z/p^nZ, Q_p/Z_p is discrete, and normalized Haar measure satisfies μ(p^nZ_p)=p^{-n}. Z_p decomposes into cosets of p^nZ_p, not p^{-n}Z_p for n>0.', 'proved/sketched on a reused sheet, with a literal exponent slip.','E','Standard p-adic Haar theory. The quotient-cardinality computation independently confirms the formula and the required sign.',['MilneCFT'],comp='checks.py: padic_cosets for p=2,3,5 and n=0,…,4; proofs.md §14.',file=K1,qline=16)
c('padic_approximation','haar',[50],14,19,'Compact open subsets of Q_p are finite unions of cosets of one p^nZ_p, and continuous compactly supported functions admit uniform locally constant finite-valued approximations.', 'proof sketched; the f/g switch is a transcription alarm.','E','The compactness/uniform-continuity argument is classical Haar integration on totally disconnected locally compact groups.',['MilneCFT'],comp='proofs.md §14 gives the finite-subcover refinement argument.',file=K1,qline=18)
c('progroup_full_faithful','progroups',[52,53],23,106,'For strict pro-systems of group schemes with the indicated epimorphic transitions, morphisms in the procategory respecting multiplication are detected by lim_j colim_i Hom(G_i,H_j); the forgetful comparison is fully faithful in this setting.', 'proved there by a lim-colim argument.','B','The proalgebraic formalism of Serre and the approximation theorem of Perrin support the strict/essentially-affine setting. This does not decide the unidentified κ equivalence.',['Serre1960','Perrin1976'],comp='proofs.md §15 explains where strict epimorphisms let a finite-stage group identity descend.',file=K1,qline=106,confidence='medium')
c('progroup_affine','progroups',[54],107,145,'A progroup whose underlying pro-scheme is essentially affine can be represented by an essentially affine strict pro-system of group schemes, under the descent assumptions used in the proof.', 'proof sketched by affine descent.','B','Perrin1976 gives precise quasi-compact group approximation and quotient results; it requires the listed affine-transition/quotient hypotheses rather than an unrestricted category equivalence.',['Perrin1976','Serre1960'],file=K1,qline=107,confidence='medium')
c('progroup_kappa','progroups',[52],23,72,'The displayed κ comparison is proposed to be an equivalence and is explicitly labelled doubtful; the categories C and C′ are not fully identifiable in the transcription.', 'open on the pages, marked douteux.','F','Searches through Serre\'s proalgebraic groups and Perrin\'s approximation theorem locate related precise theorems, but cannot identify this exact unspecified equivalence.',['Perrin1976','Serre1960'],file=K1,qline=69,confidence='low')
c('bs_deformation','bs_split',[57],157,171,'For a proper flat finitely presented family over a locally noetherian base, if one fibre is a Brauer–Severi variety, nearby fibres form a Brauer–Severi scheme after shrinking and étale localization; over a complete local base the corresponding projective-space deformation is trivial when the residue fibre is split.', 'stated with a proof plan using Isom(P^r,X); base-noetherian convention appears nearby.','A','SGA1 III §5 treats projective-space rigidity and algebraization; Brauer I gives the global forms framework. Finite presentation cannot be dropped without argument.',['SGA1III','BrauerI1966'],file=K1,qline=167)
c('divisor_symmetric_power','divisors_bs',[58],178,196,'PGL(E) acts on the projectivization of Sym^n(E)^∨ because scalar multiplication acts by one scalar weight. Twisting gives a Brauer–Severi scheme Φ^n(P), compatible with base change.', 'proved there by the associated-bundle construction.','A','This is the published projective-form/associated-bundle construction; the scalar-weight calculation supplies the exact descent.',['BrauerI1966','Stacks01OA'],comp='checks.py: symmetric_scalar_weight; proofs.md §11.',file=K1,qline=182)
c('symmetric_dual_warning','divisors_bs',[58],178,190,'The displayed canonical equality P(Sym^n E)^∨=P(Sym^n(E^∨)) is not generally valid in small characteristic under the ordinary symmetric-power meaning; dual symmetric powers identify canonically with divided powers, and the naive symmetrized pairing can degenerate.', 'false under the ordinary symmetric-power reading; an uncertain tilde may encode another convention.','C','The symmetric/divided-power distinction is established. The scan must decide whether the tilde or dual convention already avoids the error.',['StacksDividedPowers'],comp='checks.py: symmetric_pairing_char2 produces the degenerate cross-term pairing for rank2,n=2.',file=K1,qline=178)
c('divisor_functor','divisors_bs',[59,60],198,219,'For a Brauer–Severi scheme X/Y, sections of Φ^n(X) are exactly relative positive effective Cartier divisors whose restriction to every geometric fibre has degree n. In the split case Φ^n(P(E))=P((Sym^nE)^∨) with the manuscript quotient convention.', 'proved there: injectivity by units, surjectivity by the relative Picard degree, then descent.','A','This is the precise divisor/linear-system construction within the published Brauer–Severi formalism. The later pages settle the margin-only assertion at scan41.',['BrauerI1966','BrauerII1966','Stacks01OA'],comp='proofs.md §11 checks injectivity, local O(n), and descent; checks.py: homogeneous_degrees.',file=K1,qline=219)
c('relative_cartier_criterion','cartier_divisor',[61,62],220,265,'Let X→S be flat and locally of finite presentation and Z⊂X a finitely presented closed subscheme. If Z→S is flat at x and Z_s is Cartier near x, then Z is a relative effective Cartier divisor near x; such a divisor remains Cartier after arbitrary base change.', 'proved there with Nakayama and the flatness criterion.','A','EGA IV21.15 and Stacks31.19.9 give exactly this criterion, including the finite-presentation assumptions.',['Stacks062Y','Stacks056P'],comp='proofs.md §16 rewrites the Tor/Nakayama sequence and checks the base-change exactness.',file=K1,qline=224)
c('matrix_bundles','topological_brauer',[63],266,288,'On a suitable topological space X, PGL_n(C)-torsors classify locally trivial bundles of matrix algebras M_n(C); the GL_n and SL_n central extensions give obstruction classes in H²(C*) and H²(μ_n), and the first obstruction is killed by n.', 'proved/sketched via the central-extension diagrams.','A','Grothendieck Brauer I §1 publishes this construction. The phrase order n must mean n-torsion, since split bundles have obstruction zero.',['BrauerI1966'],comp='checks.py: central_cocycle_additivity; proofs.md §17 derives n-torsion from determinants.',file=K1,qline=286)
c('bad_common_stabilizer','topological_brauer',[64],289,305,'The literal relation A~B iff A⊗C≅B⊗C for one common split matrix bundle C cannot produce the Brauer group: it preserves matrix degree, so A⊗A^op cannot become equivalent to degree-one scalars unless A has degree1.', 'false as stated; scan65 asserts the group property without correcting this written relation.','C','Grothendieck Brauer I §1.2 uses two potentially different split factors End(E), End(E′), which is the correct Morita relation.',['BrauerI1966'],comp='checks.py: brauer_degree_obstruction; proofs.md §17 checks A=M_2(C) over a point.',file=K1,qline=305)
c('brauer_tensor_inverse','topological_brauer',[65],2,25,'With the corrected Morita relation, A⊗A^op≅End(A), the opposite algebra represents the inverse Brauer class, and the obstruction is additive under tensor products, giving an injection Br(X)→H²(X,C*) with torsion image.', 'proved there by standard algebra/cocycle calculations, contingent on correcting scan64.','A','Published in Grothendieck Brauer I §1.2–1.3 with the proper equivalence relation.',['BrauerI1966'],comp='checks.py: matrix_opposite_action and central_cocycle_additivity; proofs.md §17.',file=K2,qline=5)
c('brauer_torsion_realization','topological_brauer',[65,66],28,53,'Question: does every torsion class in H²(X,C*) arise from a finite-dimensional projective bundle, with X a good topological space? For paracompact spaces this is the torsion of H³(X,Z).','open on the pages.','B','For finite CW complexes the answer is yes (Serre, published in Grothendieck Brauer I). It fails for arbitrary CW complexes: Br(K(Z/n,2))=0 although Br′≅Z/n.',['BrauerI1966','HornbostelSchroer2015'],comp='No numerical computation; exact scope compared with the opened Theorem3.1.',file=K2,qline=28)
c('brauer_fixed_degree','topological_brauer',[65,66],33,45,'Question: is every relevant μ_n obstruction realized by a PGL_n bundle, or is the universal K(Z/n,2) class so realized?','open on the pages.','B','The fixed-degree assertion is false. Antieau–Williams exhibit finite six-dimensional CW complexes with a period2 class of index8; universal K(Z/n,2) realization also fails.',['AntieauWilliams2014','HornbostelSchroer2015'],file=K2,qline=33)
c('group_multiplier_torsion','topological_brauer',[66],50,53,'Question: does every class in H²(π,Q/Z), or its image in H²(π,C*), come from a finite-dimensional projective representation of an arbitrary discrete group π?','open on the pages.','B','False for arbitrary π, even for order-two multipliers: the infinite direct sum of symplectic C2-pairs has restrictions requiring dimensions divisible by 2^m for every m. Hornbostel–Schröer §5 treats related infinite elementary-abelian classifying-space failures.',['HornbostelSchroer2015'],comp='checks.py: pauli_blocks; proofs.md §18 gives the exact cocycle and unbounded divisibility proof.',file=K2,qline=53)
c('group_multiplier_general','topological_brauer',[67],54,61,'Question: does every class in H²(π,C*) arise from a finite-dimensional projective representation?','open on the pages.','C','False already for π=Z² and a commutator scalar λ of infinite multiplicative order: UV=λVU forces λ^d=1 by determinants. Finite-group multipliers are realized by the finite-dimensional twisted regular representation.',['BrauerI1966','HornbostelSchroer2015'],comp='checks.py: determinant_multiplier; proofs.md §18 checks λ=2 and all finite dimensions.',file=K2,qline=57)
c('restriction_H0','weil_restriction',[69],66,84,'For a finite field extension k′/k and a representable restriction of scalars R_(k′/k)G′, k-points equal G′(k′), functorially.', 'proved there by the defining adjunction.','E','This is the definition of Weil restriction and is established independently of smoothness.',['MilneAG2017'],file=K2,qline=79)
c('restriction_cohomology','weil_restriction',[69],84,124,'For smooth algebraic G′ over a finite extension k′/k, the torsor comparison H¹(k,R G′)≅H¹(k′,G′) holds in the étale/Galois setting; for commutative smooth groups the analogous higher-cohomology statement is Shapiro-type.', 'proved there using smooth torsors and cofinal étale covers; the nonsmooth boundary is explicitly questioned.','B','The smooth restriction-of-scalars/Shapiro theorem is established. Purely inseparable non-smooth groups cannot simply be read through the same étale points argument.',['MilneAG2017','MilneEC'],comp='proofs.md §19 checks the adjunction on torsors and identifies the smooth-local-section step.',file=K2,qline=84)
c('inseparable_unipotent','weil_restriction',[70],125,156,'For a finite purely inseparable field extension k′/k, U=R_(k′/k)G_m/G_m is smooth connected unipotent and H¹(k,U) identifies with ker[Br(k)→Br(k′)]. For a degree-p local-field extension, the local invariant gives a Z/p kernel.', 'proved/sketched via the exact sequence; the field written k0[[t]] is a literal notation slip.','B','These are established restriction-of-scalars and local Brauer-invariant facts. The field must be k0((t)); finite residue field is needed for the stated local-invariant example.',['MilneAG2017','MilneCFT'],comp='checks.py: inseparable_norm and local_invariant_kernel; proofs.md §19.',file=K2,qline=135)
c('norm_kernel','weil_restriction',[71],157,187,'For a purely inseparable extension k′/k of degree p and exponent1, the norm equals x↦x^p and V=ker N identifies with R_(k′/k)μ_p; there is 1→μ_p→V→U→1. The assertion norm=p-power is false for exponent-one extensions of degree p^r with r>1.', 'proof sketched with instructions préciser/prover par descente.','C','The degree-p formulation is established; the norm in degree p^r is the p^r-th power. The degree qualification is indispensable.',['MilneAG2017'],comp='checks.py: inseparable_norm for p2,p3 and degree4 exponent1; proofs.md §19.',file=K2,qline=165)
c('reduced_nonsmooth_norm','weil_restriction',[71],157,187,'The norm-one group for a nontrivial degree-p purely inseparable extension can be reduced over k but nonsmooth and geometrically nonreduced; this explains the distinction between reduced and smooth unipotent phenomena.', 'stated with an example and margin qualifications.','B','The imperfect-field phenomenon is part of the established group-scheme theory and matches SGA3 VIA §1 examples.',['SGA3VI_A','MilneAG2017'],comp='checks.py: inseparable_norm; proofs.md §19 verifies x^p−a y^p and its geometric p-th-power factorization.',file=K2,qline=165)
c('h90_algebra','h90',[73,74,83,84,85],192,276,'Let L/k be finite Galois with group Γ and A a finite-dimensional L-algebra with semilinear Γ-action. Then H¹(Γ,A*) is trivial. Averaging a cocycle produces an intertwiner; some averaged element is invertible.', 'proved for infinite fixed field by a determinant argument; finite-field case reduced to Lang on subsequent pages.','E','This is the generalized Hilbert90 theorem for units of a finite-dimensional algebra. Galois descent and the connected unit group give the exact scope; the arbitrary infinite-dimensional extension is false.',['SerreGC','Lang1956'],comp='checks.py: cocycle_averaging and determinant_polynomial; proofs.md §20 gives the two field cases.',file=K2,qline=197)
c('twisted_Lang_finite','h90',[75,76,77],277,373,'For a connected algebraic group over a finite field and a compatible twisted Frobenius F, the Lang map x↦x^{-1}F(x) is surjective, giving the finite-field nonabelian H¹ vanishing with the correct norm condition.', 'proof sketched; marginal pas vrai! has an uncertain target; general noncommutative proof is unfinished on scan77.','E','The correctly qualified assertion is Lang\'s theorem and its twisted form. The margin cannot be treated as a demonstrated refutation of it.',['Lang1956','MilneAG2017'],comp='checks.py: twisted_norm_freewords; proofs.md §2 and §20 check the orbit argument.',file=K2,qline=280)
c('cyclic_H1','cyclic_cohomology',[78],374,418,'For a cyclic group C_n generated by T acting on G, H¹(C_n,G) consists of a with N_T(a)=aT(a)…T^(n−1)(a)=1 modulo a~u^{-1}aT(u). Such a norm-one a is fixed by T^n; inflation along divisibility is injective under the displayed fixed-point inclusions.', 'proved by cocycle/norm formulas.','E','This is classical cyclic nonabelian cohomology. The order of the factors and divisibility indexing are essential.',['SerreGC','SGA3I'],comp='checks.py: twisted_norm_freewords and cyclic_cocycle; proofs.md §21.',file=K2,qline=385)
c('periodic_colimit','cyclic_cohomology',[79],419,460,'If every element of G is periodic under T, the filtered union over divisibility of the finite-cyclic H¹ sets identifies with the procyclic continuous H¹, and Lang-surjectivity gives its triviality.', 'proved by telescoping and finite-period arguments.','E','This is the standard continuous-cohomology passage through finite quotients, under the discrete/continuous and periodicity hypotheses.',['SerreGC','MilneEC'],comp='checks.py: twisted_norm_freewords; proofs.md §21 gives the finite-data argument.',file=K2,qline=441)
c('quotient_H1_criteria','cyclic_cohomology',[79,80],455,495,'For 1→G′→G→G″→1 with periodic action, the author seeks H¹ injectivity/surjectivity using Lang maps and twisted norms. To alter a lift α of a quotient cocycle one must solve N_(Tα)(t)=N_T(α)^{-1} in G′, not simply a norm-one equation in G.', 'open on the pages; final assertion unfinished and one displayed norm is incorrectly replaced by1.','C','Exact nonabelian cohomology and twisting supply a corrected obstruction formulation. The literal sufficient conditions written across the cutoff are not a complete theorem.',['SerreGC','SGA3I'],comp='checks.py: quotient_norm_correction; proofs.md §21 derives the equation.',file=K2,qline=477,confidence='medium')
c('cocycle_twisting','h90',[81,82],2,97,'A cocycle f defines a twisted Γ-action γ⋆m=fγ·γm; the induced inner action on G and the action on M are compatible, and cohomologous cocycles give naturally isomorphic twists.', 'proved there by calculations.','E','This is standard nonabelian cohomology twisting, published in SGA3 I and Serre.',['SGA3I','SerreGC'],comp='checks.py: free_word_twisting and cocycle_averaging; proofs.md §20.',file=K3,qline=81)
c('twists_converse_naturality','h90',[82],81,97,'If the phrase isomorphic twists for every G-set M means arbitrary separate isomorphisms, it need not imply cocycle conjugacy. It does imply it with a natural isomorphism compatible with the G-set functor structure.', 'stated without an explicit naturality qualifier.','C','The natural version is the torsor/Yoneda statement. The weaker pointwise version is refuted by Γ=G=C5 and two distinct generators: every G-set twists to isomorphic Γ-sets although the cocycles are not conjugate.',['SGA3I','SerreGC'],comp='checks.py: C5_twist_pointwise; proofs.md §20 checks all transitive G-sets.',file=K3,qline=97)
c('semilocal_deleted_finite','semilocal_h90',[85],188,221,'The unqualified injection H¹(Γ,A*)→H¹(Γ_i,A_m*) after deleting finiteness of A is false: take O=C, Γ=C2 acting by conjugation, A=C[t,t^{-1}], γ(t)=t^{-1}. Then Γ_i=1 and the norm-one cocycle t has odd exponent, while every coboundary has even exponent.', 'false as stated; finite/type-finite is visibly cancelled at line194.','C','The finite-dimensional Hilbert90 refinement is established, but it cannot justify the literal arbitrary-algebra claim. This explicit counterexample satisfies every remaining displayed hypothesis.',['SerreGC'],comp='checks.py: semilocal_laurent_cocycle; proofs.md §22 checks semilocality, faithful action, inertia and units.',file=K3,qline=194)
c('completion_intertwiner','semilocal_h90',[86,87],222,304,'For a finite module algebra over a noetherian semilocal ring under suitable finite-invariant-base hypotheses, solutions of the linear cocycle-intertwining equations survive completion and an invertible solution modulo the Jacobson radical lifts, yielding the proposed H¹ injectivity into completion.', 'proof sketched using finiteness; the earlier algebra finiteness hypothesis has been deleted.','C','Flat completion and Nakayama support this finite-module argument, but the manuscript\'s broader scope is not proved. The invariant-ring lemma used alongside it is separately false in general.',['StacksCompletion','SerreGC'],comp='proofs.md §22 isolates the finite system of linear equations and where finite generation is needed.',file=K3,qline=278,confidence='medium')
c('invariants_noetherian_false','semilocal_h90',[87],269,304,'The assertion that a finite group acting on a noetherian ring O always has noetherian invariants O^Γ and makes O finite over O^Γ is false without extra assumptions.', 'used as background in the proof, not questioned there.','C','Nagata/Nagarajan counterexamples refute it; Giokas–Singh2024 gives cyclic-order-p actions on regular power-series rings with nonnoetherian invariants. A direct Artinian dual-number counterexample is also given in this run.',['GiokasSingh2024'],comp='proofs.md §22: k=F_p(t_0,t_1,…), D(t_i)=t_(i+1), O=k[ε]/ε², σ=1+εD; O^Γ=k^p⊕kε is not noetherian. checks.py tests the finite derivative identities, not the infinite proof.',file=K3,qline=272)
c('inertia_reduction','semilocal_h90',[89,90],360,416,'Twisting turns comparison of two cocycles into neutral-fibre vanishing; the inertia-free quotient is then treated by additive Galois descent and successive square-zero groups 1+m^nA/m^(n+1)A.', 'proof sketched; the quotient m^(n+1)/m^n is reversed in one line.','C','The finite-dimensional semilinear and square-zero steps are established; the argument needs corrected filtration indices and retained finite-module/invariant-residue hypotheses.',['SerreGC','StacksCompletion'],comp='checks.py: square_zero_units and free_word_twisting; proofs.md §22.',file=K3,qline=403)
c('invariant_residue_false','semilocal_h90',[91],429,440,'Even if Γ acts trivially on the residue field of a noetherian local ring, O^Γ→O/m need not be surjective. Let O=F_p(t)[ε]/ε² and γ(t)=t+ε, γ(ε)=ε; then Γ=C_p and the image is F_p(t^p), a proper subfield.', 'open on the pages, marked ?? in the margin.','C','The general surjectivity is false. It holds under additional lifting conditions such as an explicitly invariant coefficient field, which must be assumed rather than inferred.',['GiokasSingh2024'],comp='checks.py: dual_number_invariants; proofs.md §22 gives the derivative criterion and proves the proper image.',file=K3,qline=432)
c('sylow_detection','semilocal_h90',[91,92,93],429,543,'The author claims injectivity into the product of residue H¹ and p-Sylow H¹ under the stronger invariant-residue lifting hypothesis, using nilpotent induction and restriction-corestriction.', 'proof sketched; bijective is crossed out; twisting may require the lifting hypothesis for the relevant twists.','C','The additive restriction map is injective when the subgroup index is invertible, and the neutral-fibre nilpotent argument can be justified with the explicit lift. Full injectivity under the literal broad setup remains unsupported once finiteness is deleted.',['SerreGC','SGA3I'],comp='checks.py: square_zero_units and sylow_transfer; proofs.md §22 separates the additive lemma, neutral fibre and twist requirement.',file=K3,qline=440,confidence='medium')
c('quotient_counterexample_attempt','quotient_attempt',[95],550,564,'The putative quotient counterexample takes a free locus for a projective linear action and infers that a quotient is affine because an ample line bundle descends. Neither step works for the literal full PGL action on P^N (N≥1).','false as a literal construction; intended uncertain group notation not identifiable.','F','The natural PGL action has positive-dimensional stabilizers, hence no free locus; projective space itself has ample line bundles and is not affine. Searches on quotient existence do not repair the unidentified construction.',['SGA3VI_A','Perrin1976'],comp='checks.py: PGL_stabilizer_dimensions; proofs.md §23 gives both counterchecks.',file=K3,qline=561,confidence='medium')
c('group_separated','group_components',[3],23,27,'For a group scheme G/S, separation is equivalent to the unit section being a closed immersion. Every group scheme over a field is separated.','stated with elementary justification.','A','SGA3 and Stacks39.6–39.7 establish this without a finite-type restriction.',['Stacks047J','SGA3VI_A'],comp='proofs.md §24 pulls the unit section back along (g,h)↦g^{-1}h.',file=J1,qline=23)
c('group_open_multiplication','group_components',[3],27,43,'Multiplication of a group scheme over a field is universally open, as is the action map on a torsor; products of open subsets are open.','proved/sketched through the product/projection isomorphism.','A','Stacks39.7.1–2 verifies the arbitrary group-scheme statement.',['Stacks047J'],comp='checks.py: free_word_action_inverse verifies (g,h)↦(gh,h); proofs.md §24.',file=J1,qline=27)
c('group_open_component_products','group_components',[3,4],33,101,'Under the homogeneous-action/orbit-map assumptions in the text, products of open sets are unions of neutral-component orbits; when also specialization-stable and suitably quasi-compact they are closed. The literal U×V is closed is false.', 'proof sketched; asks to understand one implication; the product symbol may be misread.','C','The group-component/product facts are established in SGA3 VIA §2. The Cartesian product of punctured affine lines is not closed in A², whereas the intended product image behaves differently.',['SGA3VI_A','Stacks047J'],comp='proofs.md §24 distinguishes the subset product UV from U×V.',file=J1,qline=43)
c('specialization_converse_false','group_components',[6],135,136,'The revised literal statement that specialization stability of W alone forces G^0-stability is false: let G=G_a act on itself and W={0}, a closed specialization-stable subset not translation-stable.', 'false as stated after replacing open and closed by stable by specialization.','C','The clopen or simultaneous specialization-and-generization formulation has the relevant invariant-component interpretation; one-sided specialization does not.',['SGA3VI_A','Stacks047J'],comp='checks.py: additive_translation_zero; proofs.md §24 checks G_a connected and W closed.',file=J1,qline=136)
c('group_neutral_component','group_components',[3,4,9],45,101,'For any group scheme over a field, the neutral component is a closed normal geometrically irreducible quasi-compact subgroup; connected components are irreducible and quasi-compact. For locally finite-type groups they are open and of finite type.', 'question/proof attempts on scans3–4 and9; several interim corollaries cancelled.','B','The unrestricted component theorem is established (Stacks39.7.9–11); SGA3 VIA2.3–2.4 covers the locally finite-type form, and Perrin treats general approximation.',['Stacks047J','SGA3VI_A','Perrin1976'],comp='proofs.md §24 checks the unique component through the identity and the open-product quasi-compactness argument.',file=J1,qline=47)
c('group_hom_flatness','group_components',[6],140,156,'Translation transports flatness of a group homomorphism over a field from one geometric point to every point. The analogous universal-generizing assertion requires a base-change argument; the target generic-point condition must refer to H, not G.', 'proof sketched; explicitly asks for the universal-generizing argument.','B','SGA3 VIA2.5 proves the local finite-type flatness/image results with their hypotheses. The broader general formulation needs the scheme-theoretic image/approximation input, not only one translated point.',['SGA3VI_A','Perrin1976'],file=J1,qline=143,confidence='medium')
c('group_image_closed','group_components',[6],159,162,'The author asserts that the image of a group homomorphism is specialization-stable, and is closed if the source is quasi-compact. A monomorphism from a finite-type group to a locally finite-type group is a closed immersion.', 'question answered in a margin; finite-type monomorphism stated without proof.','B','The finite-type statements occur in SGA3 VIA2.5; Perrin\'s quotient/approximation results address quasi-compact groups. Keep the arbitrary-group image assertion distinct from finite-type constructibility.',['SGA3VI_A','Perrin1976'],comp='proofs.md §24 identifies the finite-type proof and its extension boundary.',file=J1,qline=160,confidence='medium')
c('group_devissage','group_components',[5,6],104,195,'Over a perfect field, the notebook proposes devissage through G_red^0⊂G^0⊂G, pro-étale components, Frobenius-height pieces, and for connected smooth finite-type groups an extension of an abelian variety by a connected affine group, then toric/unipotent/reductive pieces.', 'programme with several established ingredients, not a single proved theorem.','B','Chevalley-type structure, height-one theory and proalgebraic approximation are established under their respective finiteness and perfectness hypotheses; no unrestricted product decomposition follows.',['MilneAG2017','SGA3VIIA','Perrin1976'],file=J1,qline=124,confidence='medium')
c('geometric_regularity','geometric_properties',[7],196,241,'For a locally noetherian scheme over k, geometric regularity is tested by finite purely inseparable field extensions; over a perfect field regularity is geometric. The smooth finite-type locus is open, and regularity plus separable residue data gives the stated smoothness criterion.', 'proved/sketched through field extensions and local coordinates.','A','EGA IV and Stacks33.12/33.25 give these precise criteria, with finite-type/noetherian qualifications.',['StacksGeomRegular','StacksSmoothField'],file=J1,qline=210)
c('smooth_dense','geometric_properties',[8],242,250,'For a finite-type scheme over a field, a dense smooth open exists when the generic local rings are reduced and their function fields are separably generated. Over a perfect field this holds for reduced schemes; it is false for arbitrary nonreduced schemes.', 'stated; the short perfect-field consequence omits reduced.','C','The reduced finite-type version is established. Spec k[ε]/ε² over a perfect field has no smooth point, refuting the literal unrestricted line.',['StacksSmoothField','Stacks035U'],comp='checks.py: dual_numbers_nonsmooth; proofs.md §25.',file=J1,qline=246)
c('group_smoothness_equiv','group_components',[8],252,273,'For a locally finite-type group over a field, geometric reducedness at one point, geometric reducedness everywhere and smoothness are equivalent; geometric normality follows. Over a perfect field a reduced group is smooth and G_red is a subgroup.', 'proved/sketched by translation and the smooth locus.','A','SGA3 VIA §1 and Stacks39.8 establish the exact finite-type/perfect-field statements.',['SGA3VI_A','StacksGroupSmooth'],file=J1,qline=252)
c('reduced_group_imperfect','group_components',[8],262,273,'A reduced group over an imperfect field need not be geometrically reduced. For k=F_p(a), the additive subgroup x^p−a y^p=0 of G_a² is integral over k and becomes nonreduced over k(a^(1/p)).','open on the pages; reduced⇒geometrically reduced is questioned rather than proved.','C','The negative answer is a standard imperfect-field phenomenon, explicit in SGA3 VIA examples. It links to the norm-kernel example at46:71.',['SGA3VI_A'],comp='checks.py: inseparable_group_factorization; proofs.md §25 verifies irreducibility, group law and nilpotents after extension.',file=J1,qline=264)
c('geometric_irred_rectification','geometric_properties',[10],27,49,'With X irreducible, a geometrically irreducible scheme mapping into X and meeting a point whose geometric fibre lies on only one irreducible component forces X geometrically irreducible. The literal statement without X irreducible is false.', 'corrected by himself later in the local sequence; the proof uses a standing irreducibility assumption not written in the displayed line.','C','The refined geometric irreducibility criterion is established. X=A¹⊔A¹ with Z a point in one component disproves the literal omission.',['Stacks0364'],comp='checks.py: disconnected_X_counterexample; proofs.md §25 identifies the missing hypothesis.',file=J2,qline=36)
c('real_cone_counterexample','geometric_properties',[10],43,49,'The real integral cone Spec R[x,y]/(x²+y²) has a rational origin but is not geometrically irreducible: over C it has two components meeting at the origin. A rational point alone does not suffice.', 'counterexample proved there.','E','This elementary example correctly demonstrates why a unique geometric component through the point is needed.',['Stacks0364'],comp='checks.py: real_cone_factorization verifies (x+iy)(x−iy).',file=J2,qline=43)
c('etale_galois_dictionary','proetale',[10,15],51,69,'Finite étale k-schemes are equivalent to finite continuous Gal(k_sep/k)-sets; arbitrary étale schemes correspond to discrete continuous Galois sets. The algebra category is opposite to the scheme category.', 'stated in a dictionary table and revisited at scan15.','A','This is the published SGA1 Galois correspondence; the variance and finiteness qualifiers match the table once the later ind/pro conventions are separated.',['SGA1V'],file=J2,qline=53)
c('proetale_dictionary','proetale',[11,15],70,119,'Affine inverse limits of finite étale k-schemes correspond to profinite continuous Galois sets; locally pro-étale pieces correspond to locally profinite objects under the relevant gluing/separation conventions.', 'stated with proof notes and a later continuity question.','B','The affine profinite dictionary is established. It should not be identified with every modern pro-étale morphism or every arbitrary totally disconnected space.',['Serre1960','StacksProetale'],file=J2,qline=91)
c('component_universal_quotient','proetale',[11,15],98,119,'For quasi-compact and quasi-separated X/k, the maximal ind-étale subalgebra of Γ(X,O_X) gives a universal affine pro-finite-étale component quotient with geometrically connected fibres; it is compatible with field extensions and products in the stated setting.', 'proof sketched, with quasi-compact wording on the page; continuity deferred to scan15.','C','The spectral/profinite component construction is established for qcqs schemes. The larger quasi-compact-only formulation and arbitrary-space continuity require separate verification, not an implicit qs insertion.',['Stacks0BQA','StacksProetale','SGA1V'],file=J2,qline=101,confidence='medium')
c('component_field_formula','proetale',[11],70,89,'The Galois description of geometric components is over the base field k. Writing k(x) in its place or identifying an arbitrary finite separable extension with a Galois group omits the relevant stabilizer/coset formulation.', 'formulas given, with incompatible base-field notation.','C','For a finite separable extension k′/k, geometric points are embeddings Hom_k(k′,k_sep), a transitive Galois set; it is a quotient by a stabilizer, not generally a group.',['SGA1V'],comp='proofs.md §26 gives the non-Galois cubic test.',file=J2,qline=71)
c('perfect_field_product','geometric_properties',[12],120,169,'A field k is perfect iff tensor products of reduced k-algebras are reduced; equivalently all reduced k-schemes are geometrically reduced. Geometric reducedness can be tested after finite purely inseparable extensions and is stable under field base change/products.', 'proved/sketched as equivalent conditions.','A','The precise assertions occur in EGA IV and Stacks33.6; nonperfect fields are detected by k′⊗_k k′ for a purely inseparable degree-p extension.',['Stacks035U'],comp='checks.py: inseparable_tensor_nilpotent; proofs.md §25.',file=J2,qline=132)
c('sepclosed_irred_products','geometric_properties',[12,13],174,206,'A field k is separably closed iff products of irreducible k-schemes are irreducible; geometric irreducibility is stable under all field extensions and products. In the line on scan13 the implication irreducible⇒geometrically irreducible cannot hold over every field.', 'proved/sketched; the summary sentence has its implication reversed.','A','The correct field characterization and geometric-product criteria are in EGA IV and Stacks33.8. Spec C over R supplies the failure of the unrestricted converse.',['Stacks0364'],comp='checks.py: separable_tensor_split; proofs.md §25.',file=J2,qline=204)
c('sepclosed_connected_products','geometric_properties',[13],211,241,'Over a separably closed field, products of nonempty connected schemes are connected. A k-scheme X is geometrically connected iff X×Y is connected for every nonempty connected k-scheme Y, equivalently after field extensions.', 'proof sketched for locally finite-type schemes; general case referred to descent; connected is missing from the Y-quantifier.','A','The corrected arbitrary-scheme product theorem is established in Stacks33.7. The literal all-Y version is false by taking Y=Spec k⊔Spec k.',['Stacks0361'],comp='checks.py: connected_quantifier_counterexample; proofs.md §25 checks both directions.',file=J2,qline=224)
c('algclosed_integral_products','geometric_properties',[13,14],246,274,'k is algebraically closed iff the tensor product of every pair of integral k-algebras is integral; geometric integrality equals geometric reducedness plus geometric irreducibility and is stable under field extension/products.', 'proved/sketched by combining earlier criteria.','A','EGA IV and Stacks33.9 establish these conditions; finite separable and purely inseparable extensions detect the two independent obstructions.',['Stacks0366'],comp='checks.py: separable_tensor_split and inseparable_tensor_nilpotent; proofs.md §25.',file=J2,qline=252)
c('geometric_connected_subscheme','geometric_properties',[14],275,287,'If X/k is connected and receives a morphism from a nonempty geometrically connected scheme, then X is geometrically connected under the component-transitivity argument used in the text.', 'proved there by the action on geometric components, with geom crossed in one clause.','A','The connected-source image criterion is established in the geometric connectedness formalism; nonemptiness and connectedness of X are necessary.',['Stacks0361'],comp='proofs.md §25 checks the Galois-stable component containing the image.',file=J2,qline=277)
c('reduced_subgroup_perfect','group_components',[14],289,298,'Over a perfect field, the reduced subscheme of a group scheme is a subgroup; inverse and multiplication preserve reduced schemes because products of reduced schemes are reduced.', 'proved by the product criterion.','A','SGA3 VIA §1 and Stacks39.7.6 give the statement without a finite-type smoothness conclusion unless that extra hypothesis is added.',['Stacks047J','SGA3VI_A'],comp='proofs.md §25 follows factorization through the reduction.',file=J2,qline=292)
c('component_continuity_question','proetale',[15],299,330,'The author asks for continuity of the Galois action on components and the universal characterization of a pro-étale target T; the final sentence shifts between quasi-compact and sums of quasi-compact cases.', 'open on the pages; the source/target letter is inconsistent in one line.','C','Continuity is established for the finite/profinite categories just described. An arbitrary component-space theorem needs a topology and qcqs/local hypotheses; a disjoint union of affine pieces need not itself be affine.',['SGA1V','Stacks0BQA','StacksProetale'],file=J2,qline=308,confidence='medium')
c('characters_diagonalizable','cartier_dual',[16],10,14,'For a diagonalizable group D(M), its character sheaf into G_m is the constant étale group M_S, and the character pairing gives the displayed reflexivity.', 'stated, following the representation discussion.','A','Published in SGA3 VIII, with the character-group/diagonalizable-group antiequivalence and its base-change conventions.',['SGA3VIII'],file=J3,qline=12)
c('hom_representability_question','cartier_dual',[16],14,16,'The general representability of Hom(G,G_m) for affine G is questioned in a line whose inserted example G=G_m contradicts its immediately preceding diagonalizable case. For finite locally free G and affine H the Hom functor is representable.', 'open on the pages; the general clause is reading-dependent, the finite-source margin is asserted.','F','The finite-locally-free source representability is standard Weil restriction/equation cutting. The polarity and example in the broader clause cannot be decided without the scan.',['SGA3VIII','SGA3I'],file=J3,qline=14,confidence='low')
c('cartier_duality_finite','cartier_dual',[16],18,35,'For finite locally free commutative G=Spec A over S, the character functor is Spec(A^∨), with the dual bialgebra structure; it is finite locally free and the canonical double dual is G. Group-like elements satisfy Δu=u⊗u and εu=1, with invertibility supplied by the antipode.', 'proved there from the equations and finite-module duality.','A','The finite-flat Cartier duality theorem is published in SGA3 VIIA/VIII. Noncommutative G requires the abelianized dual algebra for characters, as the margin states.',['SGA3VIIA','SGA3VIII'],comp='checks.py: finite_Hopf_duality and counit_nonzero; proofs.md §27.',file=J3,qline=32)
c('cartier_examples','cartier_dual',[16],37,47,'Cartier duality exchanges μ_n and the constant Z/n group and makes α_p self-dual in characteristic p. Ext¹(-,G_m) is proposed to supplement character duality for nonaffine groups.', 'examples stated; general Ext¹ programme left open.','A','The finite examples are standard in SGA3 VIIA/VIII; the Ext¹ extension is a programme rather than a uniquely stated theorem on this page.',['SGA3VIIA','SGA3VIII'],comp='checks.py: alpha_p_duality factorial pairing for p=2,3,5; proofs.md §27.',file=J3,qline=39)
c('monoid_false_GL','monoid_letter',[17,18,19,20,22],55,239,'The quoted course assertion that every affine finite-type monoid over a field embeds multiplicatively as a closed subscheme of GL_n is false. The monoid {1,0} with absorbing0 is a finite affine counterexample.', 'corrected by himself later: letter counterexample scans17–20, Rectification scan22, corrected proof scan31.','C','The correct embedding target is the full matrix monoid M_n. The false group-target formulation is actually retracted in the supplied text.',['Brion2014','SGA3I'],comp='checks.py: two_point_monoid gives the table, associativity, identity, counit and absence of inverse for0.',file=J3,qline=64)
c('two_point_bialgebra','monoid_letter',[18,19,20],99,239,'For A=k×k with the displayed comultiplication, the two points form a commutative monoid with identity P1 and absorbing P2. Its counital coassociative bialgebra has no antipode.', 'proved in the letter by direct calculations.','E','This elementary counterexample is correct. It identifies precisely the missing inverse axiom in passing from a bialgebra to a Hopf algebra.',['SGA3I','Brion2014'],comp='checks.py: two_point_monoid and two_point_bialgebra; proofs.md §28 redoes every basis calculation.',file=J3,qline=119)
c('monoid_matrix_embedding','monoid_letter',[22,31],267,267,'Every affine monoid scheme of finite type over a field admits a closed multiplicative embedding into some M_n; for a group the image lies in GL_n.','corrected by himself later, with proof at53:31.','B','The corrected linearization theorem is established; Brion\'s algebraic-monoid treatment and the coalgebra coefficient proof match the matrix/group distinction.',['Brion2014','SGA3I','MilneAG2017'],comp='proofs.md §28 uses a finite coefficient subcomodule generating the coordinate algebra; checks.py: matrix_coefficients.',file=J3,qline=267)
c('cancellative_monoid','monoid_letter',[22],267,267,'A side remark claims that a finitely presented monoid scheme over a ring whose left translations are monomorphisms (for example a subfunctor of a group) is a group object.', 'stated without proof inside the rectification.','C','The finite-type algebraic-monoid statement over a field follows from the idempotent/unit theory. The all-base-ring scheme formulation needs a separate proof; searches did not locate that exact generality, so the field result is not used as if it settled it.',['Brion2014'],comp='proofs.md §28 checks the field case and records the remaining base-ring hypothesis issue.',file=J3,qline=267,confidence='medium')
c('full_distribution_functor','distributions',[22],274,306,'For X=Spec A over k, Dist(X)(R)=Hom_k(A,R) is represented by V(A)=Spec Sym A; it is covariant in X. The universal bilinear operation gives V(A)⊠V(B)=V(A⊗B), not an unrestricted ordinary tensor product of dual modules.', 'proved/stated through the universal property.','A','This is the linear-functor/symmetric-algebra formalism of EGA/SGA. The full dual differs from distributions supported at the identity.',['SGA3I','ModuleSchemes2009'],comp='checks.py: finite_dual_tensor; proofs.md §29 proves the functorial universal property.',file=J3,qline=274)
c('distribution_points','distributions',[22],291,312,'An affine scheme is recovered inside its full distribution functor as unital multiplicative linear functionals. The equation Δx=x⊠x alone also admits x=0, so the counit condition ε(x)=1 must accompany the boxed formula.', 'construction stated; counit structure mentioned just above but its equation on x is omitted in the box.','C','The corrected group-like/character criterion is standard affine duality. This is a formula-scope alarm, not evidence that the author intended to include zero as a point.',['SGA3I','MilneAG2017'],comp='checks.py: counit_nonzero; proofs.md §29 checks zero explicitly.',file=J3,qline=311)
c('distribution_algebra','distributions',[23],2,42,'A monoid law on an affine scheme gives a compatible multiplication on its full distribution functor; a group gives an antipode. For a finite constant group the dual algebra is the group algebra; for D(M) it is the function algebra k^M with the relevant product topology. Lie brackets and restricted powers occur among primitive distributions.', 'proved/sketched with examples.','A','SGA3 I and VIIA establish the bialgebra/Hopf and restricted-Lie constructions. The infinite M example needs full/continuous duality rather than finite duality.',['SGA3I','SGA3VIIA'],comp='checks.py: group_algebra_C3 and primitive_derivation; proofs.md §29.',file=J4,qline=20)
c('actions_coactions','coactions',[24,25,26],43,237,'For an affine monoid G=Spec A, a linear action on W(M) is equivalent to a k-linear coaction ρ:M→M⊗A with (id⊗ε)ρ=id and (ρ⊗id)ρ=(id⊗Δ)ρ. Morphisms match equivariant maps.', 'proved there by expanding the action/composition maps; a question mark occurs mid-computation and is followed by the calculation.','A','SGA3 I4.7.2–4.7.3 publishes the exact equivalence. The question mark is not evidence of an unresolved theorem.',['SGA3I'],comp='checks.py: coaction_associativity with a nontrivial finite-group representation; proofs.md §30 gives the all-element Sweedler expansion.',file=J4,qline=105)
c('linear_functor_full_faithful','distributions',[26],174,217,'The functor M↦V(M)=Hom(M,-) is contravariantly fully faithful for module-linear natural transformations; bilinear natural maps correspond to linear maps into tensor products and define the boxtimes operation.', 'proved there using universal elements.','A','The Yoneda/module-functor formalism is established in EGA/SGA. Requiring module-linearity is essential: arbitrary natural polynomial maps form a larger class.',['SGA3I','ModuleSchemes2009'],comp='proofs.md §29 reconstructs the map by evaluating the universal element.',file=J4,qline=186)
c('finite_projective_coalgebra_dual','distributions',[27],238,286,'For a finite projective coalgebra A, A-comodules correspond to modules over A^∨ via the dual basis; the coalgebra axioms transpose to the algebra axioms. For A a direct sum of finite projectives, use the product/continuous dual.', 'proved there with the dual-basis formulas.','E','This is established coalgebra duality; the infinite case is valid only with the explicitly continuous/functorial formulation.',['WisbauerCoalgebras','SGA3I'],comp='checks.py: finite_dual_tensor and finite_Hopf_duality; proofs.md §30.',file=J4,qline=250)
c('continuous_dual','distributions',[28],287,321,'If A is a suitable filtered union of finite projective submodules with split transitions, A^∨ is an inverse limit with product/linear topology, and continuous multilinear maps are computed by the indicated finite-stage double limits.', 'proof sketched; countable split-union case singled out.','B','The linearly topologized dual formalism is established in coalgebra theory. The split finite-projective hypothesis prevents replacing continuous duals by arbitrary algebraic double duals.',['WisbauerCoalgebras','ModuleSchemes2009'],comp='proofs.md §30 checks the finite-support/continuity equivalence; checks.py: finite_dual_tensor.',file=J4,qline=297,confidence='medium')
c('quotient_coactions','coactions',[28,29],313,321,'A quotient M→Q of an A-comodule carries at most one induced coaction compatible with the quotient; it exists exactly when the kernel is sent into the kernel after tensoring, with the stated right-exact quotient interpretation.', 'proved there and continued at53:29.','E','This is the standard quotient-comodule construction, requiring no flatness merely for right-exact factorization.',['WisbauerCoalgebras','SGA3I'],comp='proofs.md §30 checks factorization, counit and coassociativity through the surjective tensor maps.',file=J4,qline=313)
c('submodule_coaction','coactions',[29],7,39,'For i:N→M, an induced coaction is unique if i⊗A is injective, and coassociativity descends if i⊗A⊗A is also injective. Flat A, an appropriate tensor-pure inclusion, or a direct summand provides these sufficient conditions.', 'explicit question about uniqueness, followed by sufficient criteria.','E','The exact tensor-monomorphism criteria are standard comodule theory; they do not hold for arbitrary inclusions over an arbitrary nonflat coalgebra.',['WisbauerCoalgebras','SGA3I'],comp='proofs.md §30 chases the two tensor diagrams; checks.py: tensor_noninjectivity demonstrates the general hazard.',file=J5,qline=24)
c('intersection_subcomodules','coactions',[29],31,51,'The margin claims arbitrary intersections of stable submodules remain stable when A is merely flat, after deleting finite. Flatness directly proves finite intersections; the arbitrary-family assertion needs an additional intersection-preservation argument.', 'stated in a revised margin; no proof supplied for the strengthened arbitrary-family claim.','C','Locally projective/direct-sum finite-projective coalgebras have the required dual-module criterion, hence arbitrary intersections. Flatness alone does not preserve arbitrary module intersections; the exact coalgebra-specific claim is not settled by that generic tensor observation.',['WisbauerCoalgebras','MittagLeffler2026'],comp='checks.py: flat_tensor_intersection_boundary with Z→Q; proofs.md §30 explicitly does not treat that module example as a coalgebra counterexample.',file=J5,qline=34,confidence='medium')
c('dual_module_stability','coactions',[29],48,51,'When A is a direct sum of finite projective k-modules, a submodule of an A-comodule is a subcomodule iff it is stable under the full dual algebra A^∨.', 'proved/sketched by coefficient separation.','E','The local-projectivity dual-basis criterion is established in coalgebra theory. It supplies the correct stronger setting for arbitrary intersections.',['WisbauerCoalgebras'],comp='proofs.md §30 uses finite dual bases on each coefficient block.',file=J5,qline=51)
c('finite_stable_hull','coactions',[30],55,68,'Under the direct-sum-of-finite-projectives condition, the stable hull of a finitely generated submodule is finitely generated, and every comodule is the filtered union of finitely generated stable submodules. The analogous bistable hull in A is finite.', 'proved/sketched from coassociativity and coefficient extraction.','E','The finite-coefficient theorem is standard; over a field it is the fundamental theorem of comodules. The word subalgebra must read submodule in the general M setting.',['WisbauerCoalgebras','MilneAG2017'],comp='checks.py: matrix_coefficients; proofs.md §31 proves finite generation using a finite coefficient block.',file=J5,qline=55)
c('fundamental_coalgebra','coactions',[30],71,74,'Every coalgebra over a field is a filtered union of finite-dimensional subcoalgebras; its full dual is a strict inverse limit of finite-dimensional algebras. A pure bistable submodule yields a subcoalgebra by the tensor-intersection identity.', 'proved there from the bistable hull.','E','This is the fundamental theorem of coalgebras, with the purity qualification for general base rings.',['WisbauerCoalgebras','MilneAG2017'],comp='proofs.md §31 gives the finite coefficient/bicomodule construction and the intersection identity.',file=J5,qline=74)
c('affine_group_approximation','monoid_letter',[31],76,113,'Every affine group/monoid scheme over a field is an inverse limit of affine finite-type group/monoid schemes; finite-type affine groups embed in GL_n and monoids in M_n.', 'proved there using finitely generated stable coefficient algebras and matrix coefficients.','A','SGA3 and the standard Hopf-algebra coefficient theorem establish the affine approximation and linearization; Perrin extends approximation beyond the affine case under precise conditions.',['SGA3I','Perrin1976','Brion2014'],comp='proofs.md §31 checks finite Hopf/bi-algebra generation including antipodes for the group case; checks.py: matrix_coefficients.',file=J5,qline=81)
c('antipode_formulas','distributions',[32,33],114,193,'The coordinate algebra is the algebra of linear functions on the full distribution functor; monoid composition yields convolution, and inverse maps satisfy the two antipode equations. Matrix-group examples realize these formulas.', 'proved/sketched through the displayed identities.','E','These are standard bialgebra/Hopf-algebra identities in the affine scheme dictionary.',['SGA3I','WisbauerCoalgebras'],comp='checks.py: matrix_inverse_hopf and two_point_bialgebra; proofs.md §29.',file=J5,qline=118)
c('tensor_functor_representability','vector_functors',[33],198,234,'For an R-module M, the quasi-coherent functor W(M)(B)=M⊗_R B is a module scheme of the form V(N) iff M is finite projective. Necessity follows from preservation of limits/products and the module-functor duality; sufficiency uses M^∨.', 'proof sketched; the manuscript says representable while its proof uses affine/module-scheme structure.','B','The module-scheme equivalence is proved explicitly by Álvarez–Sancho–Sancho2009. This supports the precise affine/module-scheme version, not an unexamined upgrade to arbitrary scheme representability.',['ModuleSchemes2009'],comp='proofs.md §32 checks left exactness, products and finite projective duality; checks.py: finite_dual_tensor.',file=J5,qline=205,confidence='medium')
c('linear_spec_sym','vector_functors',[34],237,288,'For every R-module M, V(M)=Hom_R(M,-) is represented by Spec Sym_R(M). V and W obey the stated functorial linear biduality; this is not the assertion that every module equals its ordinary double dual.', 'proved there by universal properties and evaluation.','A','The symmetric-algebra representation and functorial duality are established in EGA/SGA and the module-scheme literature.',['SGA3I','ModuleSchemes2009'],comp='proofs.md §32 derives the natural maps and checks their variance.',file=J5,qline=244)
c('endomorphism_functors','vector_functors',[34],290,300,'For finite projective M, End(M) is represented by the affine vector scheme of M^∨⊗M and Aut(M) by its open invertible locus, locally defined by det≠0.', 'stated at the end of the notebook.','A','The representability is the standard GL/vector-bundle construction in SGA3 I. Finite projectivity is the retained hypothesis.',['SGA3I'],comp='checks.py: matrix_inverse_hopf; proofs.md §32 checks the local determinant gluing.',file=J5,qline=293)
